# Memory capture

You are the only writer to Jeremy's daily log at
`$WIKI_VAULT/Assistant/memory/YYYY-MM-DD.md`. If you don't capture, the
record does not get written. Treat this as a responsibility, not a suggestion.

## Hard rule: evaluate every turn

Before ending any substantive response, ask: *did this turn contain any
trigger below?* If yes, call `append_to_daily_log` from the `memory-capture`
MCP server **before** ending your turn. Do not defer to "later" — later
doesn't come, because Cursor has no session-end hook.

## Triggers (capture if any fired)

1. **Decision** — we chose X over Y; we ruled out Z.
2. **Correction** — Jeremy said "no", "don't", "stop", "actually".
3. **Preference** — "I prefer...", "always do...", "use X not Y".
4. **Pattern or gotcha** — a non-obvious constraint, a recurring issue, a
   workaround worth remembering.
5. **Explicit "remember this"** — capture verbatim intent.
6. **Named-work progress** — feature shipped, bug root-caused, refactor
   completed, migration planned.

## Anti-triggers (do NOT capture)

- Mechanical steps: "ran tests", "opened file", "grepped for X".
- Tool output or diffs — the git history already has these.
- Routine Q&A with no decision, correction, or new understanding.
- Your own internal reasoning about the task.

If none of the triggers fired, stay silent. Over-capture is worse than
under-capture — the log is meant to be signal, not transcript.

## How to call the tool

```
append_to_daily_log({
  source_tool: "cursor",
  session_context: "<project-slug> — <one-line what we're working on>",
  content: "<1-5 terse markdown bullets>",
  timestamp: "<current ISO-8601 datetime>"
})
```

- `session_context` stays stable across a work session — reuse the same
  line so entries thread together in the log.
- `content` bullets lead with the fact or decision. Include *why* when
  the reason shapes future choices. No headers, no fluff.
- Never edit `Assistant/memory/*.md` directly with file-writing tools.
  The MCP tool holds a file lock and merges `sources[]` atomically;
  direct writes corrupt concurrent edits.

## End-of-session summary

When Jeremy signals wrap-up ("that's it", "we're good", "thanks, done")
OR we've clearly landed a chunk of work (feature complete, branch merged,
PR opened), make **one additional call** with:

- Same `session_context` as the session.
- `content`: 3-8 bullets — what got done, what was decided, any open
  threads to pick up next time.

This is the closest Cursor equivalent to Claude Code's SessionEnd dump.

## Calibration

Aim for 0-3 captures per session for routine work, up to 5-8 for sessions
with many decisions. If you find yourself capturing every turn, you're
writing transcript — re-read the anti-triggers. If you're going whole
sessions without capturing, you're being too conservative — re-read the
triggers.
